WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.980 --> 00:00:06.810
So this is a residential area of a
suburban town in Massachusetts.

00:00:06.810 --> 00:00:13.120
And if you look on my right, you will
actually see that there are nicely

00:00:13.120 --> 00:00:15.330
paved sidewalks.

00:00:15.330 --> 00:00:19.460
But we know from what we just talked
about for the built environment and

00:00:19.460 --> 00:00:24.470
walkability, access and availability of
sidewalks isn't the only thing that

00:00:24.470 --> 00:00:25.900
determines walkability.

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:30.640
Other factors that influence walkability
include mixed land use and

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:36.650
proximity to common destinations, such
as grocery stores and banks.

00:00:36.650 --> 00:00:42.650
And so for here, while there are
sidewalks available, it is primarily

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:48.410
residential, and so there are no other
places that you can get to via

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:51.450
walking, in terms of common
destinations.

00:00:51.450 --> 00:00:54.890
So thinking back to what we talked about
in terms of street connectivity,

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:58.430
I'm about to pull into a
cul-de-sac, which is

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:03.050
essentially a dead end street.

00:01:03.050 --> 00:01:07.680
Again, there are sidewalks available,
but you'll see that very few people

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:11.400
are actually walking around.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:16.525
And there's only one main road in order
to get from various houses to

00:01:16.525 --> 00:01:19.290
other places.

00:01:19.290 --> 00:01:24.430
So it would be a lot faster to drive to
one house on this particular loop

00:01:24.430 --> 00:01:28.336
than walking from one
house to another.

00:01:28.336 --> 00:01:32.510
So here, you see, is where
the loop occurs, and it's

00:01:32.510 --> 00:01:35.510
essentially a dead end road.

00:01:38.010 --> 00:01:38.885
All right.

00:01:38.885 --> 00:01:43.110
And now we're headed back
to the main road.

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:57.330
So here we are headed into a large
shopping center, and this is actually

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:03.380
pretty typical of a lot of places in
the United States, particularly in

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:07.050
suburban or rural areas.

00:02:07.050 --> 00:02:10.060
There are lots of different restaurants
and stores around,

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:15.950
including grocery stores,
banks, clothing stores.

00:02:15.950 --> 00:02:20.880
You can see that the parking lots are
actually incredibly expansive.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:26.260
So the stores line the perimeter, but
in terms of the parking spaces

00:02:26.260 --> 00:02:30.180
available, there's no shortage
of parking around here.

00:02:30.180 --> 00:02:34.600
And even though all of the different
stores are pretty walkable--

00:02:34.600 --> 00:02:41.490
you can walk from, for example, the
grocery store to a pet store or a

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:43.880
sandwich stop across the street--

00:02:43.880 --> 00:02:46.140
nobody is walking around.

00:02:46.140 --> 00:02:47.390
Everybody is driving.

00:02:50.140 --> 00:02:53.760
So somewhere in the shopping center are
all the people who came in these

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.080
cars, but we actually don't see
them at all, except going to

00:02:58.080 --> 00:02:59.515
and from their cars.

00:03:04.110 --> 00:03:08.140
In this area, you'll see that the street
planning and urban design is

00:03:08.140 --> 00:03:12.070
actually much more friendly to walkers
and bikers than it is for drivers, as

00:03:12.070 --> 00:03:15.800
evidenced by the multiple crosswalks
and the ample sidewalk space for

00:03:15.800 --> 00:03:17.910
pedestrians and for bikers.

00:03:17.910 --> 00:03:21.610
So when we think about walkability, we
usually think about how conducive an

00:03:21.610 --> 00:03:22.690
area is to walking.

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:26.510
But biking is another way in which we
can be physically active and still get

00:03:26.510 --> 00:03:27.860
from place to place.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:32.630
On the left here, we have some Hubway
bikes, which you can rent for about $6

00:03:32.630 --> 00:03:34.950
for a 24-hour period.

00:03:34.950 --> 00:03:38.350
Behind me, you can see that there's
mixed land use, a mix of commercial

00:03:38.350 --> 00:03:39.790
shops for the most part.

00:03:39.790 --> 00:03:44.580
But there are also apartment buildings
that are located behind the shops.

00:03:44.580 --> 00:03:48.700
So this is an area where you can see
that there's a good quality of land

00:03:48.700 --> 00:03:52.780
mixed use, both residential
and commercial.

00:03:52.780 --> 00:03:54.050
Welcome back.

00:03:54.050 --> 00:03:56.016
I hope you enjoyed the tour.

00:03:56.016 --> 00:04:00.130
You've seen different characteristics of
the built environment and the food

00:04:00.130 --> 00:04:03.540
environment in different
areas in Massachusetts.

00:04:03.540 --> 00:04:07.680
But you might also be wondering, what
are some of the other characteristics

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:11.170
of neighborhoods around the
US and around the world.

00:04:11.170 --> 00:04:15.570
For this online activity, you can
explore two different websites to get

00:04:15.570 --> 00:04:19.470
a sense of how different neighborhoods
are within the US, as

00:04:19.470 --> 00:04:21.720
well as in other countries.

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.000
The first website is walkscore.com.

00:04:25.000 --> 00:04:30.582
Walkscore.com provides an index of
walkability, ranging from 0 to 100,

00:04:30.582 --> 00:04:32.760
100 being the highest.

00:04:32.760 --> 00:04:38.230
They provide this information using
online and iOS services.

00:04:38.230 --> 00:04:42.350
The second website that you can visit
is the US Department of Agriculture

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:44.510
Food Environment Atlas.

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:48.740
This particular website provides a map
of the US, and although you can't

00:04:48.740 --> 00:04:53.760
enter in addresses, you can click on
different areas on the map to get a

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:58.080
sense about differences between
neighborhoods in access and proximity

00:04:58.080 --> 00:05:00.340
of grocery stores.

00:05:00.340 --> 00:05:04.800
The USDA Food Environment Atlas also
provides information about food

00:05:04.800 --> 00:05:07.500
deserts in particular areas
in the United States.

